Thomas Pornin <pornin@bolet.org> wrote:
>
> Doesn't it change gameplay in some corner cases ? One of the quirks of
> being polymorphed in a non-speaking monster is that you cannot #chat; so
> to get the price of something in a shop you have to pick it up, which is
> not a purely-UI thing in some situations (the object is a loadstone or a
> scroll of scare monster; you have teleportitis; the objet is too heavy
> to be picked up under your current form; you are polymorphed into a
> monster with no hand/paw/whatever).

Yes, there are some cases where this feature reveals more information
than in vanilla.

I think this mostly helps with price-id. But I have no problem that
price-id is easier in UnNetHack (as long as I can't remove it, it
might as well be less tedious).

The commands in vanilla NetHack are highly overloaded with (sometimes
almost useless) functionality it's almost impossible to change them
without losing something.

For example in Vanilla you can #chat to thin air and doing so maybe
reveal a hidden or invisible monster. In UnNetHack this isn't possible
if there's one monster next to the player as the code chooses
automatically that visible monster.

